<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A contact search feels instant with fifty saved people. After importing a company directory, typing one letter stalls the screen. The code is unchanged; the number of comparisons is not.</p>

Before measuring, write down what grows: contacts, characters per name, matching rows or combinations. Then count the expensive action at that scale. A complexity bound predicts growth under stated assumptions; a trace tells you whether the current implementation meets its deadline.

<!-- @after Mental Model :: guide -->
### Count the work before naming the class

For these illustrative loops, every visit or pair comparison has the same assumed cost:

| Input items N | One visit per item: N | Every item against every item: N × N |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 8 | 64 |
| 16 | 16 | 256 |
| 32 | 32 | 1,024 |

Doubling N doubles the first count and quadruples the second. This is a count of loop-body executions, not a benchmark in milliseconds. State whether you are bounding worst-case work, expected work under a distribution, or amortized work across a sequence. Those are different promises.

<!-- @after Android Example :: visual -->
### An index helps find rows; returning rows still costs work

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Query["Find users named Smith"]
    subgraph Lookup["Indexed lookup"]
      Seek["1. Seek the matching key range"]
      Match["2. Visit each of K matches"]
    end
    subgraph Output["Required result work"]
      Fetch["3. Fetch table columns if index does not cover them"]
      Return["4. Return K rows"]
    end
    Query -->|"predicate"| Seek
    Seek -->|"range found"| Match
    Match -->|"matching row identifiers"| Fetch
    Fetch -->|"requested columns"| Return
```

For the same table size N, one matching user and ten thousand matching users do not cost the same. A covering index can avoid the separate table fetch, but it cannot avoid producing the requested output. Compare the chosen query plan, selectivity and returned data before calling a query “logarithmic.”

[Source: SQLite's multiple-result and covering-index explanations](https://www.sqlite.org/queryplanner.html).

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
### Keep the count separate from the deadline

Count database calls as well as rows processed. Replacing N individual queries with one query can reduce repeated boundary overhead without making the returned N rows constant-time work. For a UI path, combine the growth bound, maximum supported input and measured elapsed cost. Moving work to a worker protects the UI thread; it does not reduce the algorithm's total work.
